---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import NewsletterForm from "../../components/forms/newsletter-form.astro";
---
<Layout title="How to Compress Video Without Losing Quality" seo={{
  page_description: "Learn how to compress video files effectively. A complete guide to bitrates, Handbrake settings, and balancing quality vs file size.",
  open_graph_type: "article"
}}>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="text-sm text-secondary mb-4">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-primary">Blog</a> &gt; How to Compress Video
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-8 capitalize">How to Compress Video Without Losing Quality</h1>

    <div class="prose max-w-none mb-12">
      <p class="lead text-xl text-secondary">
        "How do I compress a video without losing quality?" It is the most common question in digital video. The short answer: you technically can't. But you <strong>can</strong> compress it so well that the human eye cannot tell the difference.
      </p>

      <h2>The Myth of "Lossless" Compression</h2>
      <p>
        Every time you convert or compress a video into a format like <a href="/reference/mp4">MP4</a> or <a href="/reference/webm">WebM</a>, you are using a <strong>lossy</strong> codec. Algorithms throw away data (like subtle color variations in shadows) to save space. To truly compress without losing a single pixel of data, you would need a lossless codec (like ProRes 4444 or Apple Animation), which results in files hundreds of times larger than typical web video.
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead, our goal is <strong>visually lossless</strong> compression. We want to reduce the <a href="/reference/bitrate">bitrate</a> to the exact point just before blocky artifacts (macroblocking) become visible.
      </p>

      <h2>The Three Pillars of File Size</h2>
      <p>Before jumping into tools, you must understand what makes a file heavy. Your file size is determined by exactly three factors:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Resolution:</strong> The physical dimensions (e.g., 1920x1080).</li>
        <li><strong>Framerate (FPS):</strong> How many images flash per second (e.g., 30fps vs 60fps).</li>
        <li><strong>Bitrate:</strong> How much data is allowed per second of video.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        You can use our <a href="/calculators/video-file-size">Video File Size Calculator</a> to see how bitrate dictates exactly how large your final file will be.
      </p>

      <h2>The Best Settings for Handbrake (or FFmpeg)</h2>
      <p>
        If you are using an offline tool like Handbrake, or our own <a href="/">In-Browser Converter</a>, the easiest way to compress video is to use <strong>Constant Rate Factor (CRF)</strong> instead of a target bitrate.
      </p>

      <h3>What is CRF?</h3>
      <p>
        Instead of telling the encoder "use 5,000 kbps," you tell it "give me a quality level of 22." The encoder will dynamically allocate more data to complex scenes (like explosions or moving water) and less data to static scenes (like a person talking to a camera).
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>CRF Value</th>
            <th>Visual Quality</th>
            <th>File Size Impact</th>
            <th>Use Case</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>CRF 17-18</td>
            <td>Visually Lossless</td>
            <td>Very Large</td>
            <td>Archival, further editing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CRF 21-23</td>
            <td>Excellent</td>
            <td>Medium</td>
            <td><strong>Recommended default</strong> for web delivery</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CRF 27-28</td>
            <td>Acceptable</td>
            <td>Small</td>
            <td>Email attachments, Discord limits</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Upscaling resolution:</strong> Never convert a 720p video to 1080p. It will not improve quality; it will only force the encoder to stretch the pixels and waste bitrate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Converting compressed to compressed:</strong> Every time you transcode an MP4 to another MP4, generation loss occurs. Try to always compress from the original master file.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ignoring audio:</strong> Uncompressed WAV audio inside a video can add hundreds of megabytes. Ensure your audio is compressed to <a href="/reference/aac">AAC</a> at 128kbps or 256kbps.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Hardware vs Software Encoding</h2>
      <p>
        When compressing video, you might see options for NVENC (Nvidia), QuickSync (Intel), or VideoToolbox (Apple). These use your graphics card to compress video 5x to 10x faster than your CPU (x264/x265).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The Tradeoff:</strong> Hardware encoders prioritize speed over efficiency. A video compressed to 10MB using NVENC will look noticeably worse than a 10MB video compressed using CPU software encoding. If you want the absolute smallest file with the best quality, stick to CPU encoding (like x264) and be patient.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-lg border border-border">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Subscribe for more encoding deep dives</h3>
      <p class="text-secondary mb-4">We send out one technical guide a month. No spam.</p>
      <NewsletterForm form={"newsletter"} />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
